---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const focusAreas = [
  {
    title: 'Event-driven workflows',
    body: 'I prefer automation that starts from real system events, not spreadsheet glue pretending to be architecture. Core systems should own business truth. Automation should react to that truth downstream.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Workflow orchestration',
    body: 'Tools like n8n, Make, and Zapier are useful when they are treated as orchestration layers with clear triggers, explicit side effects, and visible failure paths. They are not substitutes for software design.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Applied AI workflows',
    body: 'The useful layer is usually narrow and practical: summaries, classification, routing, explanation, and decision support tied to live operational data.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Operational safety',
    body: 'Production-grade automation needs idempotency, observability, retry behavior, permission boundaries, and a way for humans to understand what happened when the flow misbehaves.',
  },
];

const principles = [
  'Keep business truth in the core system, not in the automation layer.',
  'Treat automation as downstream execution, not hidden source of record.',
  'Prefer event-driven triggers over manual polling and brittle handoffs.',
  'Use AI where it improves workflow clarity, not where it creates black-box behavior.',
  'Make automation observable enough that operators can trust it.',
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="AI Automation | Jariel Balberona"
  description="How Jariel Balberona approaches AI automation through event-driven workflows, orchestration, and applied operational tooling."
  activeNav="ai-automation"
  footerLeft="Applied automation and AI"
  footerRight="Systems first. Automation downstream."
>
  <section class="mb-20">
    <p class="mb-5 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">AI Automation</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-[16ch] text-4xl sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink leading-tight">
      I approach AI automation as systems engineering, not prompt theater.
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
      The base layer is still production software: clear boundaries, reliable data flow, explicit ownership, and operational safety. Automation and AI only become useful when they sit on top of that foundation.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base leading-relaxed text-ink">
      My focus is on event-driven workflows, orchestration, and AI-assisted tooling that reduces manual work, improves operational visibility, and supports better decisions in live systems.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mb-20 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
    {focusAreas.map((item) => (
      <article class="border-t border-line pt-5">
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">{item.title}</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">{item.body}</p>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="mb-20 border-t border-line pt-10">
    <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">Principles</p>
    <h2 class="max-w-2xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Automation should extend the system, not compete with it.</h2>
    <ul class="mt-6 space-y-3 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink list-disc marker:text-line max-w-3xl">
      {principles.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="grid gap-12 md:grid-cols-2">
    <article class="border-t border-line pt-8">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">How this connects to real systems</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Dumadine is the right kind of base layer for automation.</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Dumadine is not interesting because it covers hospitality workflows. It is interesting because it creates coherent operational state across ordering, kitchen flow, inventory, and accounting. That is what makes downstream automation and AI useful instead of forced.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Once the core system is structurally sound, you can add workflow triggers, reporting, summaries, exception routing, and decision support without inventing a second truth layer outside the product.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="border-t border-line pt-8">
      <p class="mb-3 text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">What I am not claiming</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">This is applied AI, not ML cosplay.</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        I am not presenting myself as a research engineer or ML specialist. The value here is designing production systems that can safely use automation and AI where they improve workflow, visibility, and operational decision-making.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        That is a narrower claim, but it is the honest one, and it is the one that actually ships.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
